      speaker: "",
      journal: "Crowe did not pour. The office had already chosen a screen.",
      pages: [
        "There is no bottle on the low table. That is how you know the hour has changed species. The glasses are gone. The folio is gone. In their place, a screen has been wheeled out of the wall like a tooth being shown on purpose. It is dark. The darkness is a kind of courtesy. Crowe stands beside it in a different dress, darker, less argument, more verdict. She has taken the gold chain off. The hollow of her throat looks like a place a yes could be stored.\n\n\"Close it,\" she says, as she said before. You close it. The seal takes the last of the lift's ticking and files that too. The humidifier has been turned down so the room can smell like a screen instead of citrus. You close the door on the last honest tick and feel the office become a mouth that has already chosen its meal.",
        "She does not use Rivers. She does not use {{name}} yet. She lets you stand in the unmarked space between cover and meat while the city does its melancholy work on the glass. Maren is absent. The orchid's microphone, if it still prefers stems, is alone with its stem.\n\n\"You had a productive afternoon,\" Crowe says. \"Printers. Chapels. Planters that die on schedule. I am not angry. Anger is for people who did not budget the theft. I budgeted it. I am here to show you the invoice.\"\n\nHer hand hovers over a control that is not jewelry. The screen stays dark for one more breath, a kindness so thin you could floss with it. Then she lets the dark go. Printers, chapels, planters: she recites your afternoon like a wine list. You do not correct the vintage. Correction would be a second theft, and she is about to invoice the first.",
      ],
      next: "c07_nowine",
